{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788491731,"nonce":"0q9d8Zq1GAujrUXQVtsSkUH2Nrk=","title":"The screen: advance replies with what a player would be looking at","message":"Part of the cockpit round, b4adee2. Design: RUNS/openrun-20260902/audit/COCKPIT.md\n§The screen, §When the clock stops, §How it stays honest. Depends on the\nprovenance-and-destroy spec (the gone list and the who-did-it rows come from\nits journal rows). The map panel is its own spec; until that lands the screen\ncarries the digest's site line where the map will go.\n\nWHAT\n\nA `look` verb returns the screen, and `advance` returns the same object as its\nresult, so there is nothing to read first and nothing sitting beside the thing\nthe agent asked for. Six panels, in this order: the stop line; the map; the\ncolonists; the gauges; since you last looked; decisions owed. The digest's\nexisting sections are the gauges and keep their field names.\n\nThe finding behind the shape (themes.md T0): the bridge put a truthful\n`ignored_args` block into 52 result envelopes and the agent read it zero times.\nWhat it did read, every time, was the thing it asked for — `halted_on` on an\nadvance, a refusal, `triage`'s verdict. So the screen IS the reply, and the stop\nline is its first line.\n\nSCOPE\n\n- since you last looked: the journal rows since the last screen this client\n  received, read by the mod, in three rows — game, human, mod — capped by\n  importance. A death, a downing, a destroyed building, an arrival and a letter\n  are always kept whatever the cap; `truncated` says what was dropped. The\n  last-screen mark is in memory and cleared at a game boundary, like the sampler\n  ring; the first screen after a load says \"no earlier screen\".\n- gauges: guns by def with EVERY position and coverage per gate and of the\n  inside (the map spec computes coverage; this spec prints it); power with nets\n  lacking a generator or a consumer and batteries nobody can walk to; food\n  map-wide and in stockpiles as two numbers (e811574); temperature; stock,\n  including unworn armour and uninstalled art (4c12e5d); the six contract goals\n  graded on every screen, including geysers used and unused (G2) and the armed\n  count (c41bdcc); lights — the alert readout with an age on each row and a\n  flag when an alert has cleared and returned (91bc250).\n- losses are levels: a destroyed player building stays on its gauge as \"was N\"\n  until it is rebuilt or written off with `write-off {id, reason}`, which is a\n  journaled act with a required reason. The alternative — expiry — was rejected\n  for the alert-mute ruling's reason, inverted: a loss should not quietly stop\n  being shown.\n- decisions owed: a letter with a choice; a joiner, wanderer or refugee offer;\n  an alert live for more than a day with no verdict (91bc250's item 4); a build\n  blocked on a thing the map cannot make; a chore that needs a policy it does\n  not have; a pending force-pausing dialog (9227839). Each has an id, one\n  sentence a person could answer, a deadline where the game has one, and `acts`\n  — ready-to-send envelopes, the shape `triage` already has.\n- the answer gate: `advance` is refused with `decision-owed` (class refused,\n  naming the ids) while a decision is owed. An answer is any verb that names the\n  decision id. `defer {id, reason}` is per call, the reason is required and\n  non-empty, it is journaled, and it does not clear the decision — the next\n  advance asks again. These are the three controls `unread_ok` already has.\n  This REPLACES the unread-journal gate of 722c951: the screen delivers the\n  reading, so there is no watermark to move, and a screen that arrives with the\n  reply cannot be discharged by discarding it. cd92db7 and 9dc0caa close here\n  for that reason. Until this lands, the rwa README's watermark instructions\n  stand.\n- every list on the screen is capped by importance and says what it dropped —\n  the digest's rule, applied without exception.\n- rwa writes RUNS/\u003crun\u003e/frames/\u003ctick\u003e.json for every screen (the client spec).\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- On a bench: `advance` returns a screen; `look` returns the same shape without\n  moving time; the frame file exists after each.\n- Destroy a turret with dev:destroy during an advance: the next screen lists it\n  under gone; the guns gauge reads \"was N\" on every following screen until it\n  is rebuilt or written off; the write-off is a journal row with its reason.\n- Twenty journal rows in one advance including one death: the death is in the\n  game row whatever the cap, and `truncated` names how many were dropped.\n- Spawn a raid letter with dev:incident: the next advance is refused with\n  decision-owed naming the id; answering with one of the listed acts clears it;\n  `defer` with an empty reason is bad-args; `defer` with a reason is a journal\n  row and the next advance asks again.\n- A muted alert is never a decision; an unmuted alert older than 60,000 ticks is.\n- Six turrets on the map print as six coordinates on the guns gauge, never one.\n- The screen with every cap saturated is measured in bytes and the number is\n  stated in the closing comment. COCKPIT.md estimates two to four kilobytes.\n\nABSORBS\n\ne811574 (map-wide twin), the counting half of 4c12e5d, 91bc250 (age and\nverdict), c41bdcc (armed count), cd92db7 and 9dc0caa (no read gate to defeat),\n9227839 (a pending dialog is a decision owed), the levels half of f1a1700 (its\nroom-role half is a stated limit: no durable baseline across a load — see its\ncomment #2 — so a room that changes role is UNKNOWN to this screen).","files":null}]}